Overlay patch 0061 (DECISIONS D12 = Shipwright D-084, overlay 0065):
REAL-TIME GAME-FRAME CATCH-UP + the SOH_PACE probe.

THE MECHANISM (same as every LUS port). GameEngine::ProcessGfxCommands runs
ONE MK64 game frame (30 Hz; two 60 Hz logic ticks inside it) and then renders
`count` = interpolation_fps / 30 display frames for it (120 Hz: 4). Every one
of those frames blocks on the display (vsync + LUS's nanosleep pacer) and
nothing in the loop looks at the wall clock, so a frame that misses its vsync
does not drop a frame -- it STRETCHES THE GAME FRAME: the race clock, the karts
and the sequencer's per-game-frame work all slow down with it.

THE FIX. Keep a debt: real time elapsed minus game frames x 33.3 ms (clamped
to [0, one game frame]; a stall over 250 ms resets it so the game never
fast-forwards through a pause). While the debt is at least one display frame,
drop that many of THIS game frame's EARLIEST interpolated frames (never the
last one, which is the real state). Skipping frames shortens the game frame,
which pays the debt back on the next measurement. gSohIos.TickCatchup
(default 1) is the A/B switch. The immersive modes (gSoh3DMode /
gSohVRWorldActive) are untouched -- the compositor paces those.

THE PROBE (no behaviour change): one SOH_PACE line per 10 s splitting each 2D
frame into wait (SetupScreenFramebuffer = nextDrawable), busy (drawable ->
SwapBuffersBegin: the game thread's own CPU work) and sleep (LUS's
SyncFramerateWithTime pacer), plus game-frame wall p50/p95/max ("tick" here is
the 30 Hz game frame; tps reads 30), frames skipped, debt, thermal and power
state (Low Power Mode caps the panel at 60 Hz). gSohIos.PaceStressMs (default
0, test instrument) busy-waits that many ms inside every frame so the
catch-up can be proven on a simulator.

Files: creates src/port/SohIosPace.cpp (source of truth:
overlay/assets/SohIosPace.cpp; src/port/*.cpp is globbed by CMake), hooks
gfx_metal.cpp NewFrame and gfx_sdl2.cpp SwapBuffersBegin, and calls
SohIos_PaceTick from Engine.cpp ProcessGfxCommands. Every hunk keeps clear of
earlier patches' hunks and their 3-line contexts (apply-overlay.sh re-probes
the whole series on every run).

Generate against a vendor tree with 0001..0060 applied and 0061 not applied.


